WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.984
The three checks on a cited answer.

2
00:00:02.264 --> 00:00:06.509
A citation is a claim about evidence, and you can check it at three levels.

3
00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:07.707
Provenance.

4
00:00:07.987 --> 00:00:12.104
Does the citation name a passage you actually sent on this request?

5
00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:23.733
With the citations feature this is guaranteed for live responses, but your verifier also runs on logged answers, other providers’ output, and code paths someone may change later.

6
00:00:24.013 --> 00:00:27.299
With prompt-based markers it catches invented ids.

7
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:28.432
Fidelity.

8
00:00:28.712 --> 00:00:31.635
Does the quoted text appear verbatim in that passage?

9
00:00:31.915 --> 00:00:37.461
Normalize whitespace, then require an exact substring, exactly as in Studio 1.

10
00:00:37.741 --> 00:00:46.445
Check against the cited passage, never against all passages joined together; a quote stitched across two passages would pass the joined check.

11
00:00:46.725 --> 00:00:47.600
Support.

12
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.251
Does the passage actually support the sentence it’s attached to?

13
00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:54.624
This is the hard one, and no substring check can see it.

14
00:00:54.904 --> 00:01:05.357
“Upgrades are reimbursable” attached to a passage that says “upgrades are not reimbursable unless the standard room is unavailable” passes provenance and fidelity.

15
00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:09.328
Research on citation quality shows how often support fails.

16
00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:25.331
The ALCE benchmark (Gao and colleagues, 2023) measures citation quality alongside fluency and correctness, and on its ELI5 questions even the best models of the time lacked complete citation support about half the time.

17
00:01:25.611 --> 00:01:36.448
Ragas (Es and colleagues, 2023) offers reference-free metrics, including how faithfully an answer uses its context, computed with an LLM.

18
00:01:36.728 --> 00:01:45.539
Both are judges, so treat their scores the way Studio 1 taught you to treat any judge: calibrate against labels you made yourself before trusting them.

19
00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:47.717
Studio 3 builds that harness.

20
00:01:47.997 --> 00:01:52.072
This week you build the first two checks and keep the data the third will need.
